WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.253 --> 00:00:01.670
SPEAKER 1: Throughout
this series,

00:00:01.670 --> 00:00:04.040
a lot of what we've shown
you has been focused

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:06.260
on the BigQuery
console, but when

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:09.440
you have large amounts
of data or stakeholders,

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:12.200
manually running jobs
and creating resources

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:13.520
won't be very scalable.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:14.600
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:17.900
In today's video, we're going
to take a tour of the BigQuery

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:21.780
APIs so that you can get started
creating programmatic data

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:22.280
workflows.

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:25.526
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:33.780
The first API we'll discuss
is BigQuery V2, which

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:36.690
is really the core of BigQuery.

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:39.300
Here, you can manage data
warehousing resources

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:42.810
like data sets,
tables, and routines.

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.990
You can also manage machine
learning models as well

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:49.410
as execution resources or jobs.

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:52.080
Like most of the APIs
we'll discuss today,

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.480
you can interact
with the BigQuery API

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:56.070
through the various
client libraries

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:58.170
that we have available.

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:02.250
I'm personally a Python fan, so
having the Python library makes

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.800
it so much easier for
me to programmatically

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:08.020
interact with BigQuery.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:12.000
OK, so when might you want
to actually use this API?

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.910
Well, first off, you may
want to programmatically get

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:17.850
answers to business
questions using data

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:20.370
that you have in BigQuery.

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:23.910
Let's say every week, you
want each sales team's revenue

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:27.120
to be added to a slide
deck so it can be presented

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.400
at your company's all hands.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:31.500
Well, in a Google
App Script, you

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:35.010
can write some JavaScript that
loops through each of the team

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:39.450
names, and uses them as inputs
for a parameterized query that

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:42.180
sums up their total revenue.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:45.900
We can use this information
to populate each team slide,

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.270
saving our analysts
lots of time copying

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:50.610
and pasting into the deck.

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:53.970
Alternatively, you might
want to use this API to add

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:56.680
some new data into your tables.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:59.160
For example, maybe
you have information

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:02.880
about your customers stored in
your application's Cloud SQL

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:04.380
database.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:06.360
You have your
customer addresses,

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:08.070
but you really want
to convert them

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:10.650
into lat/long
coordinates so you can

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:14.370
leverage BigQuery's awesome
geospatial capabilities.

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:18.450
Well, in this case, you can
create a Google Cloud function

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:21.780
that runs each night, and
grabs the new transactions

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:25.140
from the database using
an external query,

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.780
then uses the Google
Maps geocoding

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.570
API to grab customer
coordinates,

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:33.780
like I'm showing in
this Jupyter Notebook.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:36.330
Finally, you'll create
and run a load job

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:39.630
that appends the new data
to your transactions table.

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:41.250
So cool.

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:43.800
And on the topic
of analyzing data

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:47.850
from transactional databases,
remember a few videos ago

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:50.100
when we talked
about connections,

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.910
which allow you to write
Federated queries against Cloud

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:54.000
SQL?

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.300
Well, there's an
API for that too.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:00.900
The Connections API allows
you to programmatically create

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:04.380
and manage connections, which
can be really useful if you

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:08.040
have multiple databases
supporting your application,

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:11.340
like if you're a B2B
organization with one database

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:13.560
for each of your accounts.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.870
Here, you may want to
write a program that

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:19.470
creates a new connection as
part of the customer onboarding

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:20.850
process.

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:24.360
This way, your BigQuery
users have access to the data

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:27.910
without needing to
send a request to you.

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:31.260
You could even weave in
the Google Cloud IAM API

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:34.020
to ensure that only
users who need access

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:37.140
to the specified account's
data are able to leverage

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:38.830
the connection.

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:41.890
Besides the BigQuery
and the connections API,

00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:43.750
what else is out there?

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:47.650
Well, we talked a bit about
pushing new data into storage,

00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:52.320
but BigQuery also has a
dedicated storage API.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:55.200
The storage API
has two components.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.420
The read client
exposes a data stream

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:00.940
suitable for reading
large volumes of data.

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:04.440
This includes features for
parallelizing reads, performing

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:06.930
partial projections,
filtering data,

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:10.670
and getting precise
control over snapshot time.

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:13.040
At Google, we use
the Snapchat API

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:15.740
to build out a series
of Hadoop connectors

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:17.750
that make it easy
to use BigQuery

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:20.269
as your source of
truth data lake.

00:04:20.269 --> 00:04:24.730
You could also use it to build
out your own custom connector.

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:27.130
The Read Client,
currently in preview,

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:29.290
is the successor to
the streaming mechanism

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:32.720
found in the BigQuery V2 API.

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:34.930
It supports more
advanced write patterns,

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:38.410
such as exactly one semantics
for more robust streaming

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:39.910
inserts.

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:43.960
Keep an eye out for more
info on this coming soon.

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:47.650
Continuing on the topic of
ingesting data into BigQuery,

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:51.400
we also have the
data transfer API.

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:54.760
BigQuery Data Transfer Service
allows you to automate work

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:57.100
to ingest data
from known sources,

00:04:57.100 --> 00:05:00.400
like Cloud Storage, Google
Marketing Platform, YouTube,

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:03.010
and lots of other third
party data sources

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:05.510
on a scheduled basis.

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:08.480
With the API, you can do things
like programmatically check

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:12.110
credentials, trigger a
manual run of the transfer,

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:16.220
or get the responses
from previous transfers.

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:19.700
Now we can't have a
discussion about BigQuery APIs

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:23.060
without mentioning the
Reservation API, which

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:24.830
allows you to
manage reservations

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:27.710
and assignments like we
discussed in one of our earlier

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:29.330
videos.

00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:32.060
This is super useful for
programmatic workload

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:33.890
management.

00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:36.140
Let's say the
dashboard for your CEO

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:39.380
needs to be run every
morning at 8:00 AM.

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:41.820
Well, you want it
to be super fast,

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:43.940
so you better make sure
the project it runs in

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:46.910
will have access
to enough slots.

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:49.520
With the Reservation
Python SDK, you

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:51.740
can write a program
that runs each morning

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:54.080
and creates a new
slot reservation

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:59.500
by specifying the reservation ID
and how many slots it contains.

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:02.740
Next, you'll assign the
reservation to the designated

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:05.620
bi_project where the
dashboard queries run,

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:10.280
and specify that this
assignment is for query jobs.

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:13.860
Finally, you can delete the
assignment and reservation

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:18.190
so that slots are released
back to the organization.

00:06:18.190 --> 00:06:21.750
The last API I'll mention
is one of my favorites--

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:25.380
Data QnA, which is
currently in preview.

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:27.750
Are business users
at your organization

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:31.320
always pinging you to
query data on their behalf?

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:35.430
Well, with the QnA API, you can
convert natural language text

00:06:35.430 --> 00:06:38.430
in queries into
SQL, meaning you can

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:40.650
build a super
powerful chatbot that

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:43.860
fulfills those query requests,
or even give your business

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:46.770
users access to
Connected Sheets so they

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:51.360
can ask analytics questions
directly in a spreadsheet.

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:55.140
Wow, that's a lot of different
APIs, all under the BigQuery

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:57.480
umbrella, and we
didn't even talk

00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:00.125
through other related
products like Data Catalog.

00:07:00.125 --> 00:07:00.750
